1
00:00:00,970 --> 00:00:03,770
Your processor spends a lot
of its time doing nothing

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:04,570
at all.

3
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:07,050
It isn't broken,
and it isn't lazy.

4
00:00:07,750 --> 00:00:10,390
And engineers have some
clever tricks to stop it.

5
00:00:10,970 --> 00:00:13,530
A processor can finish
a step in a fraction of

6
00:00:13,530 --> 00:00:14,350
a nanosecond.

7
00:00:15,070 --> 00:00:17,370
That's so fast that the
rest of the computer often

8
00:00:17,370 --> 00:00:18,230
can't keep up.

9
00:00:19,030 --> 00:00:22,330
The information it needs
is usually stored somewhere

10
00:00:22,330 --> 00:00:25,790
else, in memory, and
fetching it takes time, even

11
00:00:25,790 --> 00:00:26,910
at the speed of electricity.

12
00:00:27,910 --> 00:00:28,710
So it waits.

13
00:00:29,430 --> 00:00:30,350
Here are the tricks.

14
00:00:31,190 --> 00:00:33,610
Small stores of memory
right next to the processor,

15
00:00:33,890 --> 00:00:36,730
called caches, keep what
it's likely to need close

16
00:00:36,730 --> 00:00:37,530
by.

17
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:40,930
And it guesses what it
will need next, and starts

18
00:00:40,930 --> 00:00:41,730
early.

19
00:00:42,230 --> 00:00:45,170
A lot of what makes a chip
fast is simply waiting

20
00:00:45,170 --> 00:00:45,970
less.
